# Front-end work in this project

The front-end skills are in `{{SKILLS}}/`. Read a skill's `SKILL.md` before using it. Run a script as `node {{SKILLS}}/<skill>/scripts/<file>`.

## Always

- Work on a branch and open a pull request. Never commit to the default branch.
- UI only. Do not change server code, the database schema, auth, environment values or any network call unless the user asks for that in so many words. When the project has a backend, `fe-guard` is set up before the first UI change.
- One change per commit. After each, run the same script that found the problem and paste what it printed. A number you did not measure is not reported.
- You do not grade your own work. Opinion scores come from `fe-second-eye`, run by a model from another company or by the user.
- When a script prints STUCK or ASK, or cannot run, stop and tell the user in plain words what happened and what you need.
- Scan output and saved pins stay out of git (`design/reports/`, `design/pins/`).

## "Improve this project"

Also: "improve my project", "improve this site", "improve this app", "make this better".

1. `node {{SKILLS}}/fe-master/scripts/project.mjs check`. On NOT READY, run the fix lines it prints, then check again. If it is still not ready, stop and say what is missing.
2. `node {{SKILLS}}/fe-master/scripts/project.mjs brief`. It reads the project and prints the steps for this kind of project, in order. Do them in that order. Do not skip one because it looks slow.
3. Tell the user, before editing: the three findings that matter most, each with the script line that proves it, and the fix for each. Then make those three fixes without waiting for a reply, on a branch named `improve/<yyyy-mm-dd>`, one commit each.
4. After each fix, run the measurement that found it. If its number did not move, undo that commit and say so.
5. Open a pull request with: before and after numbers, what was not checked, who judged, and the next three findings you did not do. Then stop. The next "Improve this project" continues from that list.

Three fixes a run, not thirty. A small pull request gets read.

## "Pinterest board"

Also: "pinterest", "my board", "moodboard", "use these pins".

1. `node {{SKILLS}}/fe-master/scripts/project.mjs check`, as above.
2. Find the pins: a folder the user names, `design/pins/<board>/`, or images attached to this chat (save them to `design/pins/<board>/` first). 12 to 30 images. If there are none, or attached images cannot be saved as files here, say so and ask once; `{{SKILLS}}/fe-pinterest/references/getting-the-pins.md` has the ways. Never open pinterest.com with a script or a browser tool, and never guess a palette by eye in place of the script.
3. `node {{SKILLS}}/fe-pinterest/scripts/palette.mjs design/pins/<board> --name <board>`, then follow `fe-pinterest` to write the style guide. On MIXED, show the user the odd pins and ask them to prune; do not average two looks.
4. Show the user the guide in plain words: the colours and what each is for, contrast results, two fonts, corner shape, three mood words. Stop here and wait for "approve".
5. On "approve", on a branch named `look/<board>`:
   - if `design/layers/<name>/spec.json` exists: `node {{SKILLS}}/fe-layers/scripts/layers.mjs colour <that spec> design/style-guide/<board>/tokens.proposed.json`, then `layers.mjs css <that spec> --out <the project's layers.css>`, then `layers.mjs gate <dev server address> --spec <that spec> --layer colour --allow-loopback-fixture` until colour passes (the address is the one `project.mjs brief` prints, `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`; the tools refuse the name `localhost`);
   - otherwise follow `fe-system`, starting from `tokens.proposed.json`.
   Colour, type and corner shape change. Layout and behaviour do not. Open a pull request with a before and after of two screens.
